   [\fB\-march=\fR\fIarch\fR] [\fB\-mbig\-endian\fR|\fB\-mlittle\-endian\fR]
   [\fB\-mdsbt\fR|\fB\-mno\-dsbt\fR] [\fB\-mpid=no\fR|\fB\-mpid=near\fR|\fB\-mpid=far\fR]
   [\fB\-mpic\fR|\fB\-mno\-pic\fR]
.PP
\&\fITarget TILE\-Gx options:\fR
   [\fB\-m32\fR|\fB\-m64\fR][\fB\-EB\fR][\fB\-EL\fR]
.PP
\&\fITarget Visium options:\fR
   [\fB\-mtune=\fR\fIarch\fR]
.PP
\&\fITarget Xtensa options:\fR
 [\fB\-\-[no\-]text\-section\-literals\fR] [\fB\-\-[no\-]auto\-litpools\fR]
 [\fB\-\-[no\-]absolute\-literals\fR]
 [\fB\-\-[no\-]target\-align\fR] [\fB\-\-[no\-]longcalls\fR]
 [\fB\-\-[no\-]transform\fR]
 [\fB\-\-rename\-section\fR \fIoldname\fR=\fInewname\fR]
 [\fB\-\-[no\-]trampolines\fR]
 [\fB\-\-abi\-windowed\fR|\fB\-\-abi\-call0\fR]
.PP
\&\fITarget Z80 options:\fR
  [\fB\-march=\fR\fICPU\fR\fI[\-EXT]\fR\fI[+EXT]\fR]
  [\fB\-local\-prefix=\fR\fIPREFIX\fR]
  [\fB\-colonless\fR]
  [\fB\-sdcc\fR]
  [\fB\-fp\-s=\fR\fIFORMAT\fR]
  [\fB\-fp\-d=\fR\fIFORMAT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
GNU \fBas\fR is really a family of assemblers.
If you use (or have used) the GNU assembler on one architecture, you
should find a fairly similar environment when you use it on another
architecture.  Each version has much in common with the others,
including object file formats, most assembler directives (often called
\&\fIpseudo\-ops\fR) and assembler syntax.
.PP
\&\fBas\fR is primarily intended to assemble the output of the
GNU C compiler \f(CR\*(C`gcc\*(C'\fR for use by the linker
\&\f(CR\*(C`ld\*(C'\fR.  Nevertheless, we\*(Aqve tried to make \fBas\fR
assemble correctly everything that other assemblers for the same
machine would assemble.
Any exceptions are documented explicitly.
This doesn\*(Aqt mean \fBas\fR always uses the same syntax as another
assembler for the same architecture; for example, we know of several
incompatible versions of 680x0 assembly language syntax.
.PP
Each time you run \fBas\fR it assembles exactly one source
program.  The source program is made up of one or more files.
(The standard input is also a file.)
.PP
You give \fBas\fR a command line that has zero or more input file
names.  The input files are read (from left file name to right).  A
command\-line argument (in any position) that has no special meaning
is taken to be an input file name.
.PP
If you give \fBas\fR no file names it attempts to read one input file
from the \fBas\fR standard input, which is normally your terminal.  You
may have to type \fBctl\-D\fR to tell \fBas\fR there is no more program
to assemble.
.PP
Use \fB\-\-\fR if you need to explicitly name the standard input file
in your command line.
.PP
If the source is empty, \fBas\fR produces a small, empty object
file.
.PP
\&\fBas\fR may write warnings and error messages to the standard error
file (usually your terminal).  This should not happen when  a compiler
runs \fBas\fR automatically.  Warnings report an assumption made so
that \fBas\fR could keep assembling a flawed program; errors report a
grave problem that stops the assembly.
.PP
If you are invoking \fBas\fR via the GNU C compiler,
you can use the \fB\-Wa\fR option to pass arguments through to the assembler.
The assembler arguments must be separated from each other (and the \fB\-Wa\fR)
by commas.  For example:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&        gcc \-c \-g \-O \-Wa,\-alh,\-L file.c
.Ve
.PP
This passes two options to the assembler: \fB\-alh\fR (emit a listing to
standard output with high\-level and assembly source) and \fB\-L\fR (retain
local symbols in the symbol table).
.PP
Usually you do not need to use this \fB\-Wa\fR mechanism, since many compiler
command\-line options are automatically passed to the assembler by the compiler.
(You can call the GNU compiler driver with the \fB\-v\fR option to see
precisely what options it passes to each compilation pass, including the
assembler.)
.SH OPTIONS
.IX Header "OPTIONS"
.TP 4
\&\fB@\fR\fIfile\fR
.IX Item "@file"
Read command\-line options from \fIfile\fR.  The options read are
inserted in place of the original @\fIfile\fR option.  If \fIfile\fR
does not exist, or cannot be read, then the option will be treated
literally, and not removed.
.Sp
Options in \fIfile\fR are separated by whitespace.  A whitespace
character may be included in an option by surrounding the entire
option in either single or double quotes.  Any character (including a
backslash) may be included by prefixing the character to be included
with a backslash.  The \fIfile\fR may itself contain additional
@\fIfile\fR options; any such options will be processed recursively.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-a[cdghilmns]\fR
.IX Item "-a[cdghilmns]"
Turn on listings, in any of a variety of ways:
.RS 4
.TP 4
\&\fB\-ac\fR
.IX Item "-ac"
omit false conditionals
.TP 4
\&\fB\-ad\fR
.IX Item "-ad"
omit debugging directives
.TP 4
\&\fB\-ag\fR
.IX Item "-ag"
include general information, like as version and options passed
.TP 4
\&\fB\-ah\fR
.IX Item "-ah"
include high\-level source
.TP 4
\&\fB\-al\fR
.IX Item "-al"
include assembly
.TP 4
\&\fB\-ali\fR
.IX Item "-ali"
include assembly with ginsn
.TP 4
\&\fB\-am\fR
.IX Item "-am"
include macro expansions
.TP 4
\&\fB\-an\fR
.IX Item "-an"
omit forms processing
.TP 4
\&\fB\-as\fR
.IX Item "-as"
include symbols
.TP 4
\&\fB=file\fR
.IX Item "=file"
set the name of the listing file
.RE
.RS 4
.Sp
You may combine these options; for example, use \fB\-aln\fR for assembly
listing without forms processing.  The \fB=file\fR option, if used, must be
the last one.  By itself, \fB\-a\fR defaults to \fB\-ahls\fR.
.RE
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-alternate\fR
.IX Item "--alternate"
Begin in alternate macro mode.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections\fR
.IX Item "--compress-debug-sections"
Compress DWARF debug sections using zlib with SHF_COMPRESSED from the
ELF ABI.  The resulting object file may not be compatible with older
linkers and object file utilities.  Note if compression would make a
given section \fIlarger\fR then it is not compressed.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=none\fR
.IX Item "--compress-debug-sections=none"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zlib\fR
.IX Item "--compress-debug-sections=zlib"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zlib\-gnu\fR
.IX Item "--compress-debug-sections=zlib-gnu"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zlib\-gabi\fR
.IX Item "--compress-debug-sections=zlib-gabi"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zstd\fR
.IX Item "--compress-debug-sections=zstd"
.PD
These options control how DWARF debug sections are compressed.
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=none\fR is equivalent to
\&\fB\-\-nocompress\-debug\-sections\fR.
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zlib\fR and
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zlib\-gabi\fR are equivalent to
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections\fR.
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zlib\-gnu\fR compresses DWARF debug sections
using the obsoleted zlib\-gnu format.  The debug sections are renamed to begin
with \fB.zdebug\fR.
\&\fB\-\-compress\-debug\-sections=zstd\fR compresses DWARF debug
sections using zstd.  Note \- if compression would actually make a section
\&\fIlarger\fR, then it is not compressed nor renamed.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-nocompress\-debug\-sections\fR
.IX Item "--nocompress-debug-sections"
Do not compress DWARF debug sections.  This is usually the default for all
targets except the x86/x86_64, but a configure time option can be used to
override this.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-D\fR
.IX Item "-D"
Enable debugging in target specific backends, if supported.  Otherwise ignored.
Even if ignored, this option is accepted for script compatibility with calls to
other assemblers.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-debug\-prefix\-map\fR \fIold\fR\fB=\fR\fInew\fR
.IX Item "--debug-prefix-map old=new"
When assembling files in directory \fIold\fR, record debugging
information describing them as in \fInew\fR instead.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-defsym\fR \fIsym\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR
.IX Item "--defsym sym=value"
Define the symbol \fIsym\fR to be \fIvalue\fR before assembling the input file.
\&\fIvalue\fR must be an integer constant.  As in C, a leading \fB0x\fR
indicates a hexadecimal value, and a leading \fB0\fR indicates an octal
value.  The value of the symbol can be overridden inside a source file via the
use of a \f(CR\*(C`.set\*(C'\fR pseudo\-op.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-dump\-config\fR
.IX Item "--dump-config"
Displays how the assembler is configured and then exits.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-elf\-stt\-common=no\fR
.IX Item "--elf-stt-common=no"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-elf\-stt\-common=yes\fR
.IX Item "--elf-stt-common=yes"
.PD
These options control whether the ELF assembler should generate common
symbols with the \f(CR\*(C`STT_COMMON\*(C'\fR type.  The default can be controlled
by a configure option \fB\-\-enable\-elf\-stt\-common\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-emulation=\fR\fIname\fR
.IX Item "--emulation=name"
If the assembler is configured to support multiple different target
configurations then this option can be used to select the desired form.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-f\fR
.IX Item "-f"
"fast"\-\-\-skip whitespace and comment preprocessing (assume source is
compiler output).
.TP 4
\&\fB\-g\fR
.IX Item "-g"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gen\-debug\fR
.IX Item "--gen-debug"
.PD
Generate debugging information for each assembler source line using whichever
debug format is preferred by the target.  This currently means either STABS,
ECOFF or DWARF2.  When the debug format is DWARF then a \f(CR\*(C`.debug_info\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CR\*(C`.debug_line\*(C'\fR section is only emitted when the assembly file doesn\*(Aqt
generate one itself.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gstabs\fR
.IX Item "--gstabs"
Generate stabs debugging information for each assembler line.  This
may help debugging assembler code, if the debugger can handle it.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gstabs+\fR
.IX Item "--gstabs+"
Generate stabs debugging information for each assembler line, with GNU
extensions that probably only gdb can handle, and that could make other
debuggers crash or refuse to read your program.  This
may help debugging assembler code.  Currently the only GNU extension is
the location of the current working directory at assembling time.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gdwarf\-2\fR
.IX Item "--gdwarf-2"
Generate DWARF2 debugging information for each assembler line.  This
may help debugging assembler code, if the debugger can handle it.  Note\-\-\-this
option is only supported by some targets, not all of them.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gdwarf\-3\fR
.IX Item "--gdwarf-3"
This option is the same as the \fB\-\-gdwarf\-2\fR option, except that it
allows for the possibility of the generation of extra debug information as per
version 3 of the DWARF specification.  Note \- enabling this option does not
guarantee the generation of any extra information, the choice to do so is on a
per target basis.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gdwarf\-4\fR
.IX Item "--gdwarf-4"
This option is the same as the \fB\-\-gdwarf\-2\fR option, except that it
allows for the possibility of the generation of extra debug information as per
version 4 of the DWARF specification.  Note \- enabling this option does not
guarantee the generation of any extra information, the choice to do so is on a
per target basis.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gdwarf\-5\fR
.IX Item "--gdwarf-5"
This option is the same as the \fB\-\-gdwarf\-2\fR option, except that it
allows for the possibility of the generation of extra debug information as per
version 5 of the DWARF specification.  Note \- enabling this option does not
guarantee the generation of any extra information, the choice to do so is on a
per target basis.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gdwarf\-sections\fR
.IX Item "--gdwarf-sections"
Instead of creating a .debug_line section, create a series of
\&.debug_line.\fIfoo\fR sections where \fIfoo\fR is the name of the
corresponding code section.  For example a code section called \fI.text.func\fR
will have its dwarf line number information placed into a section called
\&\fI.debug_line.text.func\fR.  If the code section is just called \fI.text\fR
then debug line section will still be called just \fI.debug_line\fR without any
suffix.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gdwarf\-cie\-version=\fR\fIversion\fR
.IX Item "--gdwarf-cie-version=version"
Control which version of DWARF Common Information Entries (CIEs) are produced.
When this flag is not specified the default is version 1, though some targets
can modify this default.  Other possible values for \fIversion\fR are 3 or 4.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-generate\-missing\-build\-notes=yes\fR
.IX Item "--generate-missing-build-notes=yes"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-generate\-missing\-build\-notes=no\fR
.IX Item "--generate-missing-build-notes=no"
.PD
These options control whether the ELF assembler should generate GNU Build
attribute notes if none are present in the input sources.
The default can be controlled by the \fB\-\-enable\-generate\-build\-notes\fR
configure option.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gsframe\fR
.IX Item "--gsframe"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-gsframe\fR
.IX Item "--gsframe"
.PD
Create \fI.sframe\fR section from CFI directives.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-hash\-size\fR \fIN\fR
.IX Item "--hash-size N"
Ignored.  Supported for command line compatibility with other assemblers.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-help\fR
.IX Item "--help"
Print a summary of the command\-line options and exit.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-target\-help\fR
.IX Item "--target-help"
Print a summary of all target specific options and exit.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-I\fR \fIdir\fR
.IX Item "-I dir"
Add directory \fIdir\fR to the search list for \f(CR\*(C`.include\*(C'\fR directives.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-J\fR
.IX Item "-J"
Don\*(Aqt warn about signed overflow.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-K\fR
.IX Item "-K"
Issue warnings when difference tables altered for long displacements.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-L\fR
.IX Item "-L"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-keep\-locals\fR
.IX Item "--keep-locals"
.PD
Keep (in the symbol table) local symbols.  These symbols start with
system\-specific local label prefixes, typically \fB.L\fR for ELF systems
or \fBL\fR for traditional a.out systems.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-listing\-lhs\-width=\fR\fInumber\fR
.IX Item "--listing-lhs-width=number"
Set the maximum width, in words, of the output data column for an assembler
listing to \fInumber\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-listing\-lhs\-width2=\fR\fInumber\fR
.IX Item "--listing-lhs-width2=number"
Set the maximum width, in words, of the output data column for continuation
lines in an assembler listing to \fInumber\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-listing\-rhs\-width=\fR\fInumber\fR
.IX Item "--listing-rhs-width=number"
Set the maximum width of an input source line, as displayed in a listing, to
\&\fInumber\fR bytes.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-listing\-cont\-lines=\fR\fInumber\fR
.IX Item "--listing-cont-lines=number"
Set the maximum number of lines printed in a listing for a single line of input
to \fInumber\fR + 1.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-multibyte\-handling=allow\fR
.IX Item "--multibyte-handling=allow"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-multibyte\-handling=warn\fR
.IX Item "--multibyte-handling=warn"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-multibyte\-handling=warn\-sym\-only\fR
.IX Item "--multibyte-handling=warn-sym-only"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-multibyte\-handling=warn_sym_only\fR
.IX Item "--multibyte-handling=warn_sym_only"
.PD
Controls how the assembler handles multibyte characters in the input.  The
default (which can be restored by using the \fBallow\fR argument) is to
allow such characters without complaint.  Using the \fBwarn\fR argument will
make the assembler generate a warning message whenever any multibyte character
is encountered.  Using the \fBwarn\-sym\-only\fR argument will only cause a
warning to be generated when a symbol is defined with a name that contains
multibyte characters.  (References to undefined symbols will not generate a
warning).
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-no\-pad\-sections\fR
.IX Item "--no-pad-sections"
Stop the assembler for padding the ends of output sections to the alignment
of that section.  The default is to pad the sections, but this can waste space
which might be needed on targets which have tight memory constraints.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-o\fR \fIobjfile\fR
.IX Item "-o objfile"
Name the object\-file output from \fBas\fR \fIobjfile\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-R\fR
.IX Item "-R"
Fold the data section into the text section.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-reduce\-memory\-overheads\fR
.IX Item "--reduce-memory-overheads"
Ignored.  Supported for compatibility with tools that apss the same option to
both the assembler and the linker.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-scfi=experimental\fR
.IX Item "--scfi=experimental"
This option controls whether the assembler should synthesize CFI for
hand\-written input.  If the input already contains some synthesizable CFI
directives, the assembler ignores them and emits a warning.  Note that
\&\f(CR\*(C`\-\-scfi=experimental\*(C'\fR is not intended to be used for compiler\-generated
code, including inline assembly.  This experimental support is work in
progress.  Only System V AMD64 ABI is supported.
.Sp
Each input function in assembly must begin with the \f(CR\*(C`.type\*(C'\fR directive, and
should ideally be closed off using a \f(CR\*(C`.size\*(C'\fR directive.  When using SCFI,
each \f(CR\*(C`.type\*(C'\fR directive prompts GAS to start a new FDE (a.k.a., Function
Descriptor Entry).  This implies that with each \f(CR\*(C`.type\*(C'\fR directive, a
previous block of instructions, if any, is finalised as a distinct FDE.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-sectname\-subst\fR
.IX Item "--sectname-subst"
Honor substitution sequences in section names.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-size\-check=error\fR
.IX Item "--size-check=error"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-size\-check=warning\fR
.IX Item "--size-check=warning"
.PD
Issue an error or warning for invalid ELF .size directive.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-statistics\fR
.IX Item "--statistics"
Print the maximum space (in bytes) and total time (in seconds) used by
assembly.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-strip\-local\-absolute\fR
.IX Item "--strip-local-absolute"
Remove local absolute symbols from the outgoing symbol table.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-v\fR
.IX Item "-v"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-version\fR
.IX Item "-version"
.PD
Print the \fBas\fR version.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-version\fR
.IX Item "--version"
Print the \fBas\fR version and exit.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-W\fR
.IX Item "-W"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-no\-warn\fR
.IX Item "--no-warn"
.PD
Suppress warning messages.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-fatal\-warnings\fR
.IX Item "--fatal-warnings"
Treat warnings as errors.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-warn\fR
.IX Item "--warn"
Don\*(Aqt suppress warning messages or treat them as errors.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-w\fR
.IX Item "-w"
Ignored.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-x\fR
.IX Item "-x"
Ignored.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-Z\fR
.IX Item "-Z"
Generate an object file even after errors.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\- |\fR \fIfiles\fR \fB...\fR
.IX Item "-- | files ..."
Standard input, or source files to assemble.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for the
64\-bit mode of the ARM Architecture (AArch64).
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EB\fR
.IX Item "-EB"
This option specifies that the output generated by the assembler should
be marked as being encoded for a big\-endian processor.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EL\fR
.IX Item "-EL"
This option specifies that the output generated by the assembler should
be marked as being encoded for a little\-endian processor.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mabi=\fR\fIabi\fR
.IX Item "-mabi=abi"
Specify which ABI the source code uses.  The recognized arguments
are: \f(CR\*(C`ilp32\*(C'\fR and \f(CR\*(C`lp64\*(C'\fR, which decides the generated object
file in ELF32 and ELF64 format respectively.  The default is \f(CR\*(C`lp64\*(C'\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcpu=\fR\fIprocessor\fR\fB[+\fR\fIextension\fR\fB...]\fR
.IX Item "-mcpu=processor[+extension...]"
This option specifies the target processor.  The assembler will issue an error
message if an attempt is made to assemble an instruction which will not execute
on the target processor.  The following processor names are recognized:
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a34\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a35\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a53\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a55\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a57\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a65\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a65ae\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a72\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a73\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a75\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a76\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a76ae\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a77\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a78\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a78ae\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a78c\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a510\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a520\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a710\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-a720\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ares\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`exynos\-m1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`falkor\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`neoverse\-n1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`neoverse\-n2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`neoverse\-e1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`neoverse\-v1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`qdf24xx\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`saphira\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`thunderx\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`vulcan\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`xgene1\*(C'\fR
\&\f(CR\*(C`xgene2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-r82\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-x1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-x2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-x3\*(C'\fR,
and
\&\f(CR\*(C`cortex\-x4\*(C'\fR.
The special name \f(CR\*(C`all\*(C'\fR may be used to allow the assembler to accept
instructions valid for any supported processor, including all optional
extensions.
.Sp
In addition to the basic instruction set, the assembler can be told to
accept, or restrict, various extension mnemonics that extend the
processor.
.Sp
If some implementations of a particular processor can have an
extension, then then those extensions are automatically enabled.
Consequently, you will not normally have to specify any additional
extensions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-march=\fR\fIarchitecture\fR\fB[+\fR\fIextension\fR\fB...]\fR
.IX Item "-march=architecture[+extension...]"
This option specifies the target architecture.  The assembler will
issue an error message if an attempt is made to assemble an
instruction which will not execute on the target architecture.  The
following architecture names are recognized: \f(CR\*(C`armv8\-a\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`armv8.1\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8.2\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8.3\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8.4\-a\*(C'\fR
\&\f(CR\*(C`armv8.5\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8.6\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8.7\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8.8\-a\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`armv8.9\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv8\-r\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv9\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv9.1\-a\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`armv9.2\-a\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`armv9.3\-a\*(C'\fR and \f(CR\*(C`armv9.4\-a\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
If both \fB\-mcpu\fR and \fB\-march\fR are specified, the
assembler will use the setting for \fB\-mcpu\fR.  If neither are
specified, the assembler will default to \fB\-mcpu=all\fR.
.Sp
The architecture option can be extended with the same instruction set
extension options as the \fB\-mcpu\fR option.  Unlike
\&\fB\-mcpu\fR, extensions are not always enabled by default.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mverbose\-error\fR
.IX Item "-mverbose-error"
This option enables verbose error messages for AArch64 gas.  This option
is enabled by default.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-verbose\-error\fR
.IX Item "-mno-verbose-error"
This option disables verbose error messages in AArch64 gas.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for an Alpha
processor.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-m\fR\fIcpu\fR
.IX Item "-mcpu"
This option specifies the target processor.  If an attempt is made to
assemble an instruction which will not execute on the target processor,
the assembler may either expand the instruction as a macro or issue an
error message.  This option is equivalent to the \f(CR\*(C`.arch\*(C'\fR directive.
.Sp
The following processor names are recognized:
\&\f(CR21064\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`21064a\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR21066\fR,
\&\f(CR21068\fR,
\&\f(CR21164\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`21164a\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`21164pc\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR21264\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`21264a\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`21264b\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev4\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev5\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`lca45\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev5\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev56\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`pca56\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev6\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev67\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`ev68\*(C'\fR.
The special name \f(CR\*(C`all\*(C'\fR may be used to allow the assembler to accept
instructions valid for any Alpha processor.
.Sp
In order to support existing practice in OSF/1 with respect to \f(CR\*(C`.arch\*(C'\fR,
and existing practice within \fBMILO\fR (the Linux ARC bootloader), the
numbered processor names (e.g. 21064) enable the processor\-specific PALcode
instructions, while the "electro\-vlasic" names (e.g. \f(CR\*(C`ev4\*(C'\fR) do not.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mdebug\fR
.IX Item "-mdebug"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-no\-mdebug\fR
.IX Item "-no-mdebug"
.PD
Enables or disables the generation of \f(CR\*(C`.mdebug\*(C'\fR encapsulation for
stabs directives and procedure descriptors.  The default is to automatically
enable \f(CR\*(C`.mdebug\*(C'\fR when the first stabs directive is seen.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-relax\fR
.IX Item "-relax"
This option forces all relocations to be put into the object file, instead
of saving space and resolving some relocations at assembly time.  Note that
this option does not propagate all symbol arithmetic into the object file,
because not all symbol arithmetic can be represented.  However, the option
can still be useful in specific applications.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-replace\fR
.IX Item "-replace"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-noreplace\fR
.IX Item "-noreplace"
.PD
Enables or disables the optimization of procedure calls, both at assemblage
and at link time.  These options are only available for VMS targets and
\&\f(CR\*(C`\-replace\*(C'\fR is the default.  See section 1.4.1 of the OpenVMS Linker
Utility Manual.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-g\fR
.IX Item "-g"
This option is used when the compiler generates debug information.  When
\&\fBgcc\fR is using \fBmips\-tfile\fR to generate debug
information for ECOFF, local labels must be passed through to the object
file.  Otherwise this option has no effect.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-G\fR\fIsize\fR
.IX Item "-Gsize"
A local common symbol larger than \fIsize\fR is placed in \f(CR\*(C`.bss\*(C'\fR,
while smaller symbols are placed in \f(CR\*(C`.sbss\*(C'\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-F\fR
.IX Item "-F"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-32addr\fR
.IX Item "-32addr"
.PD
These options are ignored for backward compatibility.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for an ARC
processor.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcpu=\fR\fIcpu\fR
.IX Item "-mcpu=cpu"
This option selects the core processor variant.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EB | \-EL\fR
.IX Item "-EB | -EL"
Select either big\-endian (\-EB) or little\-endian (\-EL) output.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcode\-density\fR
.IX Item "-mcode-density"
Enable Code Density extension instructions.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for the ARM
processor family.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcpu=\fR\fIprocessor\fR\fB[+\fR\fIextension\fR\fB...]\fR
.IX Item "-mcpu=processor[+extension...]"
Specify which ARM processor variant is the target.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-march=\fR\fIarchitecture\fR\fB[+\fR\fIextension\fR\fB...]\fR
.IX Item "-march=architecture[+extension...]"
Specify which ARM architecture variant is used by the target.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mfpu=\fR\fIfloating\-point\-format\fR
.IX Item "-mfpu=floating-point-format"
Select which Floating Point architecture is the target.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mfloat\-abi=\fR\fIabi\fR
.IX Item "-mfloat-abi=abi"
Select which floating point ABI is in use.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mthumb\fR
.IX Item "-mthumb"
Enable Thumb only instruction decoding.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mapcs\-32 | \-mapcs\-26 | \-mapcs\-float | \-mapcs\-reentrant\fR
.IX Item "-mapcs-32 | -mapcs-26 | -mapcs-float | -mapcs-reentrant"
Select which procedure calling convention is in use.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EB | \-EL\fR
.IX Item "-EB | -EL"
Select either big\-endian (\-EB) or little\-endian (\-EL) output.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mthumb\-interwork\fR
.IX Item "-mthumb-interwork"
Specify that the code has been generated with interworking between Thumb and
ARM code in mind.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mccs\fR
.IX Item "-mccs"
Turns on CodeComposer Studio assembly syntax compatibility mode.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-k\fR
.IX Item "-k"
Specify that PIC code has been generated.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for
the Blackfin processor family.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcpu=\fR\fIprocessor\fR[\fB\-\fR\fIsirevision\fR]
.IX Item "-mcpu=processor[-sirevision]"
This option specifies the target processor.  The optional \fIsirevision\fR
is not used in assembler.  It\*(Aqs here such that GCC can easily pass down its
\&\f(CR\*(C`\-mcpu=\*(C'\fR option.  The assembler will issue an
error message if an attempt is made to assemble an instruction which
will not execute on the target processor.  The following processor names are
recognized:
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf504\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf506\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf512\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf514\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf516\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf518\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf522\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf523\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf524\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf525\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf526\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf527\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf531\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf532\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf533\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf534\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf535\*(C'\fR (not implemented yet),
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf536\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf537\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf538\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf539\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf542\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf542m\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf544\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf544m\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf547\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf547m\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf548\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf548m\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf549\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf549m\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf561\*(C'\fR,
and
\&\f(CR\*(C`bf592\*(C'\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mfdpic\fR
.IX Item "-mfdpic"
Assemble for the FDPIC ABI.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-fdpic\fR
.IX Item "-mno-fdpic"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mnopic\fR
.IX Item "-mnopic"
.PD
Disable \-mfdpic.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for
the Linux kernel BPF processor family.
.PP
\&\f(CR@chapter\fR BPF Dependent Features
.SS "BPF Options"
.IX Subsection "BPF Options"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EB\fR
.IX Item "-EB"
This option specifies that the assembler should emit big\-endian eBPF.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EL\fR
.IX Item "-EL"
This option specifies that the assembler should emit little\-endian
eBPF.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mdialect=\fR\fIdialect\fR
.IX Item "-mdialect=dialect"
This option specifies the assembly language dialect to recognize while
assembling.  The assembler supports \fBnormal\fR and
\&\fBpseudoc\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-misa\-spec=\fR\fIspec\fR
.IX Item "-misa-spec=spec"
This option specifies the version of the BPF instruction set to use
when assembling.  The BPF ISA versions supported are \fBv1\fR \fBv2\fR, \fBv3\fR and \fBv4\fR.
.Sp
The value \fBxbpf\fR can be specified to recognize extra
instructions that are used by GCC for testing purposes.  But beware
this is not valid BPF.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-relax\fR
.IX Item "-mno-relax"
This option tells the assembler to not relax instructions.
.PP
Note that if no endianness option is specified in the command line,
the host endianness is used.
See the info pages for documentation of the CRIS\-specific options.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for
the C\-SKY processor family.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-march=\fR\fIarchname\fR
.IX Item "-march=archname"
Assemble for architecture \fIarchname\fR.  The \fB\-\-help\fR option
lists valid values for \fIarchname\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcpu=\fR\fIcpuname\fR
.IX Item "-mcpu=cpuname"
Assemble for architecture \fIcpuname\fR.  The \fB\-\-help\fR option
lists valid values for \fIcpuname\fR.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EL\fR
.IX Item "-EL"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mlittle\-endian\fR
.IX Item "-mlittle-endian"
.PD
Generate little\-endian output.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-EB\fR
.IX Item "-EB"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mbig\-endian\fR
.IX Item "-mbig-endian"
.PD
Generate big\-endian output.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-fpic\fR
.IX Item "-fpic"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-pic\fR
.IX Item "-pic"
.PD
Generate position\-independent code.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mljump\fR
.IX Item "-mljump"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-ljump\fR
.IX Item "-mno-ljump"
.PD
Enable/disable transformation of the short branch instructions
\&\f(CR\*(C`jbf\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`jbt\*(C'\fR, and \f(CR\*(C`jbr\*(C'\fR to \f(CR\*(C`jmpi\*(C'\fR.
This option is for V2 processors only.
It is ignored on CK801 and CK802 targets, which do not support the \f(CR\*(C`jmpi\*(C'\fR
instruction, and is enabled by default for other processors.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mbranch\-stub\fR
.IX Item "-mbranch-stub"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-branch\-stub\fR
.IX Item "-mno-branch-stub"
.PD
Pass through \f(CR\*(C`R_CKCORE_PCREL_IMM26BY2\*(C'\fR relocations for \f(CR\*(C`bsr\*(C'\fR
instructions to the linker.
.Sp
This option is only available for bare\-metal C\-SKY V2 ELF targets,
where it is enabled by default.  It cannot be used in code that will be
dynamically linked against shared libraries.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-force2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-force2bsr"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mforce2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-mforce2bsr"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-no\-force2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-no-force2bsr"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-force2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-mno-force2bsr"
.PD
Enable/disable transformation of \f(CR\*(C`jbsr\*(C'\fR instructions to \f(CR\*(C`bsr\*(C'\fR.
This option is always enabled (and \fB\-mno\-force2bsr\fR is ignored)
for CK801/CK802 targets.  It is also always enabled when
\&\fB\-mbranch\-stub\fR is in effect.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-jsri2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-jsri2bsr"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mjsri2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-mjsri2bsr"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-no\-jsri2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-no-jsri2bsr"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-jsri2bsr\fR
.IX Item "-mno-jsri2bsr"
.PD
Enable/disable transformation of \f(CR\*(C`jsri\*(C'\fR instructions to \f(CR\*(C`bsr\*(C'\fR.
This option is enabled by default.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mnolrw\fR
.IX Item "-mnolrw"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-lrw\fR
.IX Item "-mno-lrw"
.PD
Enable/disable transformation of \f(CR\*(C`lrw\*(C'\fR instructions into a
\&\f(CR\*(C`movih\*(C'\fR/\f(CR\*(C`ori\*(C'\fR pair.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-melrw\fR
.IX Item "-melrw"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-elrw\fR
.IX Item "-mno-elrw"
.PD
Enable/disable extended \f(CR\*(C`lrw\*(C'\fR instructions.
This option is enabled by default for CK800\-series processors.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mlaf\fR
.IX Item "-mlaf"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mliterals\-after\-func\fR
.IX Item "-mliterals-after-func"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-laf\fR
.IX Item "-mno-laf"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-literals\-after\-func\fR
.IX Item "-mno-literals-after-func"
.PD
Enable/disable placement of literal pools after each function.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mlabr\fR
.IX Item "-mlabr"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mliterals\-after\-br\fR
.IX Item "-mliterals-after-br"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-labr\fR
.IX Item "-mno-labr"
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mnoliterals\-after\-br\fR
.IX Item "-mnoliterals-after-br"
.PD
Enable/disable placement of literal pools after unconditional branches.
This option is enabled by default.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mistack\fR
.IX Item "-mistack"
.PD 0
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mno\-istack\fR
.IX Item "-mno-istack"
.PD
Enable/disable interrupt stack instructions.  This option is enabled by
default on CK801, CK802, and CK802 processors.
.PP
The following options explicitly enable certain optional instructions.
These features are also enabled implicitly by using \f(CR\*(C`\-mcpu=\*(C'\fR to specify
a processor that supports it.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mhard\-float\fR
.IX Item "-mhard-float"
Enable hard float instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mmp\fR
.IX Item "-mmp"
Enable multiprocessor instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcp\fR
.IX Item "-mcp"
Enable coprocessor instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mcache\fR
.IX Item "-mcache"
Enable cache prefetch instruction.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-msecurity\fR
.IX Item "-msecurity"
Enable C\-SKY security instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mtrust\fR
.IX Item "-mtrust"
Enable C\-SKY trust instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mdsp\fR
.IX Item "-mdsp"
Enable DSP instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-medsp\fR
.IX Item "-medsp"
Enable enhanced DSP instructions.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mvdsp\fR
.IX Item "-mvdsp"
Enable vector DSP instructions.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for
an Epiphany processor.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mepiphany\fR
.IX Item "-mepiphany"
Specifies that the both 32 and 16 bit instructions are allowed.  This is the
default behavior.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mepiphany16\fR
.IX Item "-mepiphany16"
Restricts the permitted instructions to just the 16 bit set.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for an H8/300
processor.
\&\f(CR@chapter\fR H8/300 Dependent Features
.SS Options
.IX Subsection "Options"
The Renesas H8/300 version of \f(CR\*(C`as\*(C'\fR has one
machine\-dependent option:
.TP 4
\&\fB\-h\-tick\-hex\fR
.IX Item "-h-tick-hex"
Support H\*(Aq00 style hex constants in addition to 0x00 style.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-mach=\fR\fIname\fR
.IX Item "-mach=name"
Sets the H8300 machine variant.  The following machine names
are recognised:
\&\f(CR\*(C`h8300h\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`h8300hn\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`h8300s\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`h8300sn\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`h8300sx\*(C'\fR and 
\&\f(CR\*(C`h8300sxn\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The following options are available when as is configured for
an i386 processor.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-32 | \-\-x32 | \-\-64\fR
.IX Item "--32 | --x32 | --64"
Select the word size, either 32 bits or 64 bits.  \fB\-\-32\fR
implies Intel i386 architecture, while \fB\-\-x32\fR and \fB\-\-64\fR
imply AMD x86\-64 architecture with 32\-bit or 64\-bit word\-size
respectively.
.Sp
These options are only available with the ELF object file format, and
require that the necessary BFD support has been included (on a 32\-bit
platform you have to add \-\-enable\-64\-bit\-bfd to configure enable 64\-bit
usage and use x86\-64 as target platform).
.TP 4
\&\fB\-n\fR
.IX Item "-n"
By default, x86 GAS replaces multiple nop instructions used for
alignment within code sections with multi\-byte nop instructions such
as leal 0(%esi,1),%esi.  This switch disables the optimization if a single
byte nop (0x90) is explicitly specified as the fill byte for alignment.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-\-divide\fR
.IX Item "--divide"
On SVR4\-derived platforms, the character \fB/\fR is treated as a comment
character, which means that it cannot be used in expressions.  The
\&\fB\-\-divide\fR option turns \fB/\fR into a normal character.  This does
not disable \fB/\fR at the beginning of a line starting a comment, or
affect using \fB#\fR for starting a comment.
.TP 4
\&\fB\-march=\fR\fICPU\fR\fB[+\fR\fIEXTENSION\fR\fB...]\fR
.IX Item "-march=CPU[+EXTENSION...]"
This option specifies the target processor.  The assembler will
issue an error message if an attempt is made to assemble an instruction
which will not execute on the target processor.  The following
processor names are recognized:
\&\f(CR\*(C`i8086\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`i186\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`i286\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`i386\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`i486\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`i586\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`i686\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`pentium\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`pentiumpro\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`pentiumii\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`pentiumiii\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`pentium4\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`prescott\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`nocona\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`core\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`core2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`corei7\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`iamcu\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`k6\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`k6_2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`athlon\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`opteron\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`k8\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`amdfam10\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bdver1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bdver2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bdver3\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`bdver4\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`znver1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`znver2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`znver3\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`znver4\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`znver5\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`btver1\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`btver2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`generic32\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CR\*(C`generic64\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
In addition to the basic instruction set, the assembler can be told to
accept various extension mnemonics.  For example,
\&\f(CR\*(C`\-march=i686+sse4+vmx\*(C'\fR extends \fIi686\fR with \fIsse4\fR and
\&\fIvmx\fR.  The following extensions are currently supported:
\&\f(CR8087\fR,
\&\f(CR287\fR,
\&\f(CR387\fR,
\&\f(CR687\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`cmov\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`fxsr\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`mmx\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CR\*(C`sse\*(C'\fR,
